WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.040
Film Courage: Where does the rule don’t
be boring come into play?

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:10.970
UCLA Screenwriting chair Richard Walter: From
the beginning to end with the least we want

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:15.400
is to not be bored when I got to the movies
and see screeners (scandalously were are seeing

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.740
less and less and looking at more and more
screeners).

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:24.220
I used to feel guilty not going to the theaters
now I feel guilty not screening the screeners

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.070
and the reason is that I am bored.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:30.430
I think most art is pretty bad, most paintings,
most sculpture, most literature and music

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.430
is lousy.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:37.821
Now that might seem like a cynical and dark
thing to say but it’s the opposite of that.

00:00:37.821 --> 00:00:43.000
The truth is that the stuff that is contemporary,
much of it is going to be pretty bad.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.770
Some of it will be brilliant.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:49.540
What happens is the bad stuff falls away.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:54.230
I was in New York not too terribly long ago
and at the Metropolitan Museum of Art (one

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.680
of my favorite museums) and I didn’t see
a single painting there that wasn’t timeless

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:00.809
and eternal that didn’t deserve to be there
and you could get the impression from that

00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:03.250
that paintings are some how worthy all of
the time.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:06.840
But what we don’t realize all of the time
(and what a lot of people don’t realize)

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.170
is that for every painting that hangs at the
Met there are 10,000 worthless, jerky, amateur

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.170
paintings.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:16.409
Now I think it’s wonderful that people try
to paint and try to be creative and take a

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.789
shot at it but it’s unusual that it’s
worth bothering someone else about.

00:01:19.789 --> 00:01:24.389
That is to say there is something in the work
that merits the time and consideration of

00:01:24.389 --> 00:01:25.529
an audience (an observer).

00:01:25.529 --> 00:01:30.939
In film because it is contemporary, because
it is in our face now there hasn’t been

00:01:30.939 --> 00:01:36.740
a chance for the bad stuff to drop away so
we think it’s uniquely bad when it isn’t

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.880
and it’s just as bad as other art.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:45.829
Fortunately the little bit of really, really
good stuff is enough to change your life and

00:01:45.829 --> 00:01:48.779
make it worth engaging so that you do see
all of the good stuff.

00:01:48.779 --> 00:01:54.079
People say “Why don’t they make good movies
now like in the 30’s and the 40’s!”

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:57.819
Did they make good movies now like in the
30’s and the 40’s?

00:01:57.819 --> 00:02:00.289
They did, there were a lot of good ones.

00:02:00.289 --> 00:02:02.200
But there were also a lot of lousy ones.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.200
We just don’t remember them.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.280
I think 50 or 60 years from now they will
be saying “Why don’t they make good movies

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.410
now like in they did in the early New Millennium.”

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:11.480
Because people will have forgotten THE HUNGER
GAMES.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:22.370
I’m not 
a big fan of THE HUNGER GAMES, I think people

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:23.810
are going to forget about them.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:27.200
But they are going to remember THE IMITATION
GAME (the “games” today I guess).

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:33.790
Isn’t that the movie about touring and so
on.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:46.609
So the least we want, when I say don’t be
boring, that’s the very least I want from

00:02:46.609 --> 00:03:01.530
a work of art we are talking now about dramatic
narrative.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:08.129
I was at the Louvre in Paris and I saw the
Mona Lisa among others.

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:16.840
How much time did I spend with it, a minute?

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:24.700
If I had spent three minutes with it that
would have been a long time.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:39.060
If I had sat quietly for just a few seconds
I would feel how heavily time ways.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:48.439
I’m just waiting for…see if I get quiet
for just two seconds you get nervous and you

00:03:48.439 --> 00:03:51.200
jump in…see that’s my point.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.849
Film Courage: Okay…sorry.

00:03:52.849 --> 00:03:59.849
I’m part of the experiment.

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:01.110
Richard Walter:

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.060
That’s okay, you are helping me.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:15.849
But that’s the most we want, that’s the
least we want.

00:04:15.849 --> 00:04:41.419
I have a deal with my wife which is if I start
to nod out during the movie, she’s supposed

00:04:41.419 --> 00:04:54.580
to let me do that unless I’m snoring and
wheezing and spraying everybody around me

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:58.210
and bringing disgrace and discredit and humiliation
upon.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:40.370
It’s up to the movie to keep me awake and
the fact of the matter is that I used to fight

00:05:40.370 --> 00:06:06.919
it if I started to drift during the movie,
I used to fight it like “Hey!”

00:06:06.919 --> 00:06:11.430
And take a deep breath and pay attention.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:16.300
Now if I start to drift, that’s it!

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.280
I’m going to nod out on this one.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:20.729
It isn’t worth seeing.

00:06:20.729 --> 00:06:35.569
I’ve walked into movies where I was really
weary, hadn’t slept very well but it

00:06:35.569 --> 00:07:12.270
was 
a good movie and it woke me up.

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:20.520
So that’s the least we want.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:23.360
Don’t be boring.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:38.870
And if that’s all you do, that’s an incredible
achievement.

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:44.720
But again that’s the bottom.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:51.169
What’s the top?

00:07:51.169 --> 00:08:12.490
And the top is I want my life changed completely
the way great art does.

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:20.180
I want it turned upside and transmogrified.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:27.740
I want to be transported and that’s what
BREAKING BAD did to me, that’s what THE

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:28.740
SOPRANO’s did to me.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:40.290
My life is different constantly because of
that exposure to that great art.

00:08:40.290 --> 00:08:57.690
And that’s what great art should do at the
top end.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:04.760
It should just change your life forever.

